Add command registration after kanban.createTask command (around line 308):
```typescript
context.subscriptions.push(
  vscode.commands.registerCommand('kanban.findTask', async () => {
    const tasks = loadAllTasks();

    if (tasks.length === 0) {
      vscode.window.showInformationMessage('No tasks found');
      return;
    }

    const items = tasks.map(task => ({
      label: `${task.id}: ${task.title}`,
      description: task.labels.map(l => `#${l}`).join(' '),
      detail: `Status: ${task.status} | Priority: ${task.priority || 'none'}`,
      task,
    }));

    const selected = await vscode.window.showQuickPick(items, {
      placeHolder: 'Search tasks by ID or title...',
      matchOnDescription: true,
      matchOnDetail: true,
    });

    if (selected) {
      // Reveal task in tree view (implemented in next step)
    }
  })
);
```

- After QuickPick selection, find the TaskItem for the selected task
- Need to get TaskItem from the TreeDataProvider to reveal it
- TreeView.reveal() requires the actual TreeItem instance
- Add a method to tasks-view capability to find TaskItem by task ID
- Call treeView.reveal(taskItem, { expand: true, select: true, focus: true })

The challenge: treeView.reveal() needs the actual TaskItem instance, not just task data.

Solution: Create TaskItems for all tasks in the status group, find the matching one.

In the findTask command handler, after selection:
```typescript
if (selected) {
  // Load tasks and find the TaskItem
  const statusTasks = loadAllTasks().filter(t => t.status === selected.task.status);
  const taskItems = statusTasks.map(t =>
    new TaskItem(t, checkTaskFiles(t.taskPath), getAllActions(t))
  );
  const taskItem = taskItems.find(item => item.task.id === selected.task.id);

  if (taskItem) {
    // First reveal the status group to expand it
    const columns = taskGrouping.getColumns();
    const column = columns.find(c => c.id === selected.task.status);
    if (column) {
      const grouped = taskGrouping.groupByStatus(loadAllTasks());
      const count = (grouped.get(column.id) || []).length;
      const statusGroup = new StatusGroupItem(column.id, column.name, count, false);
      await treeView.reveal(statusGroup, { expand: true });
    }

    // Then reveal the task
    await treeView.reveal(taskItem, { select: true, focus: true });
  }
}
```

Issue: StatusGroupItem and TaskItem are created fresh, so reveal may not work since
TreeView compares by reference. Need to implement getParent() in TreeDataProvider
or use a different approach.

Better approach: Export a getTaskItemById helper from tasks-view capability that
returns a TaskItem given a task ID. The TreeDataProvider can cache items.

Alternative (simpler): TreeView.reveal() can work with freshly created items if
we implement a proper element equality check. But VSCode TreeView doesn't support
custom equality.

Final approach: Have the findTask command:
1. Find the StatusGroupItem for the task's status in the tree
2. Expand it using reveal()
3. Find the TaskItem within that group
4. Reveal it

Since we need the actual TreeItem instances from the provider, we'll need to either:
- Make tasks-view capability expose a method to get items by task ID
- Or recreate items that match what the provider creates

The simplest working approach: Re-use the same TreeItem creation logic and trust
that reveal() will match by structural equality for the expand/select to work.

Actually, VSCode TreeView.reveal() uses the same reference internally if the item
comes from getChildren(). We need to ensure we're using items from the provider.

Simplest fix: Add a findTaskItem(taskId) method to tasks-view capability that
returns a TaskItem or undefined. This way we get the same reference.

- Export StatusGroupItem class (currently private)
- Add findTaskItem(taskId: string) method to capability return type
- Add findStatusGroup(status: TaskStatus) method to capability return type
- These methods return the same instances used by getChildren()

Problem: Current implementation creates new TaskItem instances each time getTasksForStatus
is called. To support reveal(), we need to cache the items.

Solution: Add caching layer to the capability:

```typescript
// Add cache at capability level
let cachedStatusGroups: StatusGroupItem[] | null = null;
let cachedTaskItems: Map = new Map();

function getStatusGroups(): StatusGroupItem[] {
  // ... existing logic ...
  cachedStatusGroups = groups;
  return groups;
}

function getTasksForStatus(status: TaskStatus): TaskItem[] {
  const tasks = deps.loadTasks();
  const items = tasks
    .filter((t) => t.status === status)
    .map((t) => new TaskItem(t, deps.checkTaskFiles(t.taskPath), deps.getAllActions(t)));

  // Cache items
  items.forEach(item => cachedTaskItems.set(item.task.id, item));

  return items;
}

function findTaskItem(taskId: string): TaskItem | undefined {
  // First check cache
  if (cachedTaskItems.has(taskId)) {
    return cachedTaskItems.get(taskId);
  }

  // If not cached, load all tasks to populate cache
  const tasks = deps.loadTasks();
  for (const task of tasks) {
    if (!cachedTaskItems.has(task.id)) {
      cachedTaskItems.set(task.id,
        new TaskItem(task, deps.checkTaskFiles(task.taskPath), deps.getAllActions(task)));
    }
  }

  return cachedTaskItems.get(taskId);
}

function findStatusGroup(status: TaskStatus): StatusGroupItem | undefined {
  if (!cachedStatusGroups) {
    getStatusGroups(); // Populate cache
  }
  return cachedStatusGroups?.find(g => g.status === status);
}

function createRefreshCallback(): () => void {
  return () => {
    // Clear caches on refresh
    cachedStatusGroups = null;
    cachedTaskItems.clear();
    onDidChangeTreeData.fire();
  };
}
```

Update return type:
```typescript
export interface CreateTasksViewCapabilityReturn {
  createTreeDataProvider(): vscode.TreeDataProvider;
  createRefreshCallback(): () => void;
  findTaskItem(taskId: string): TaskItem | undefined;
  findStatusGroup(status: TaskStatus): StatusGroupItem | undefined;
}
```

